# How to cite this software, in the format GitHub reads.
#
# The audience for this product is academic, and attribution in that world
# is a citation rather than a line in a footer. GitHub renders this file as
# a "Cite this repository" button and produces APA and BibTeX from it, so a
# reader who wants to credit the software gets the credit right without
# having to guess at a form. The licence already requires the copyright
# notice to be kept; this asks for the thing that is actually worth more,
# and it asks rather than requires.
#
# Citation File Format 1.2.0. Its schema
# (https://github.com/citation-file-format/citation-file-format) requires
# exactly four keys -- `cff-version`, `message`, `title`, `authors` -- and
# refuses any key it does not know, so everything below is either required
# or a documented optional key.
# `tools/tests/test_public_repository.py` holds this file against that
# required set and against the licence the repository actually ships.
#
# No `repository-code` and no `url`, deliberately. A duplicate inherits
# this file, and an address here would send that duplicate's readers to
# upstream's repository as though it were the software they ran. What the
# citation names instead is the work and its author, which stays true in a
# duplicate that has not modified the software -- and a duplicate that has
# modified it adds its own author and its own address, which is what the
# licence asks of it anyway.
#
# No `version` and no `date-released`, for a plainer reason: there are no
# releases. A fix lands on the default branch and reaches an instance
# through the merge it already does, so a version here would name something
# nobody can check out.
cff-version: 1.2.0
message: >-
  If you use this software, or run a seminar series on it, please cite it
  using the metadata in this file.
title: Convener
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: GIRARD
    given-names: Benoît J. T.
license: AGPL-3.0-or-later
abstract: >-
  Convener runs a scholarly seminar series end to end -- proposals, an
  editorial board, invitations and scheduling, registration, attendance
  matching, verifiable certificates, and the destruction of participants'
  personal data on a deadline -- with no database, no server and no running
  cost. The repository is the store, continuous integration is the compute,
  and three small edge workers hold what has to answer continuously. It is
  written for volunteer-run scholarly societies, where the work is unpaid,
  the person doing it changes every year or two, and there is no budget to
  lose.
keywords:
  - seminar series
  - research software
  - scholarly society
  - open access
  - attendance certificate
  - data protection
  - continuous integration
